<section xml:id="CommonFactor">
  <title>Factoring: Common Factor</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      What is a factor?
    </p>
    <p>
      When it comes to multiplying, a <em>factor</em>
          <idx><h>Factor</h></idx>
      is one of the things being multiplied and the <em>product</em>
      is the result that is obtained from multiplying.
      For example in the equation <m>2 \cdot 6 = 12</m>,
      <m>2</m> and <m>6</m> are factors and the product is <m>12</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="Distribution">section</xref>;
      the distributive law gives us <m>a(b+c)=ab+ac</m>.
      Here <m>a</m> and <m>b+c</m> are the factors and the product is <m>ab+ac</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Putting this into a context,
      we have <m>3(x+5)=3x+15</m> with factors <m>3</m> and <m>(x+5)</m> and a product of <m>3x+15</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection>
    <title>Common Factor</title>
    <p>
      We can use a common factor to reverse the distributive law we were introduced to in <xref ref="Distribution">section</xref>.
      A common factor is a term that divides each term of the expression.
      We are looking for our result to be
      <em>factored completely</em> ,
          <idx><h>Factor</h><h>Completely</h></idx>
      meaning that there are no other factors that can be divided out.
      When looking at the example above,
      <m>3</m> is a common factor of the expression <m>3x+15</m> because <m>3</m> divides both <m>3x</m> and <m>15</m> evenly.
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>10x^2+25x</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are looking for a common factor that divides both <m>10x^2</m> and <m>25x</m>. Beginning with the coefficients, <m>5</m> divides both <m>10</m> and <m>25</m>. The highest power of <m>x</m> that divides both <m>x^2</m> and <m>x</m> is <m>x</m>. Therefore, our greatest common factor (GCF) is <m>5x</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          When we divide both terms in our original expression by <m>5x</m>, we have:
          <me>
            \frac{10x^2}{5x} + \frac{25x}{5x} = \frac{10 \cdot x \cdot x}{5 \cdot x} + \frac{25 \cdot x}{5 \cdot x} = 2x + 5
          </me>
            Thus, we obtain the factored form:
          <me>
            10x^2+25x=5x(2x+5)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the terms <m>2x</m> and <m>5</m> share no other common factors (other than 1), we have completely factored our expression. We can use the distributive law to check our work:
          <me>
            5x(2x+5)=5x(2x)+5x(5)=10x^2+25x
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since multiplying the factors <m>5x</m> and <m>2x+5</m> returns us to the original expression, the factorization is correct.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>4x^3+20x^2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are looking for a common factor that divides both <m>4x^3</m> and <m>20x^2</m>. Beginning with the coefficients, <m>2</m> is a common factor of <m>4</m> and <m>20</m>, and the highest power of <m>x</m> that divides both <m>x^3</m> and <m>x^2</m> is <m>x^2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If we use the common factor <m>2x^2</m> and divide both terms in our original expression, we have:
          <me>
            \frac{4x^3}{2x^2} + \frac{20x^2}{2x^2} = \frac{4 \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x}{2 \cdot x \cdot x} + \frac{20 \cdot x \cdot x}{2 \cdot x \cdot x} = 2x + 10
          </me>
          The factorization obtained is <m>4x^3+20x^2=2x^2(2x+10)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          However, the terms inside the parentheses share another common factor of <m>2</m>, so our result is <term>not completely factored</term>. We would then have to factor out another <m>2</m>:
          <me>
            4x^3+20x^2=2x^2(2x+10)=2x^2(2)(x+5)=4x^2(x+5)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice that the final common factor becomes <m>4x^2</m>. If we had originally divided both terms by <m>4x^2</m>, we would have reached <m>4x^2(x+5)</m> in one step.
        </p>
        <p>
          Although we get the same result, it is more efficient to factor out <m>4x^2</m> initially. We call <m>4x^2</m> the <term>greatest common factor (GCF)</term> of <m>4x^3</m> and <m>20x^2</m> because it has the largest coefficient and the highest degree literal part that divides both terms.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>3x+21</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beginning with the coefficients, <m>3</m> divides both <m>3</m> and <m>21</m>. Because <m>21</m> has no literal part, the variable cannot be a part of our common factor. Therefore, our greatest common factor is <m>3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          When we divide both terms in our original expression by <m>3</m>, we have:
          <me>
            \frac{3x}{3} + \frac{21}{3} = x + 7
          </me>
          Thus, we obtain the factored form:
          <me>
            3x+21=3(x+7)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the terms <m>x</m> and <m>7</m> share no other common factors, our expression is factored completely.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>-6x+9</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both <m>-6</m> and <m>9</m> are divisible by <m>3</m>. Since the second term has no literal part, the variable will not be included in our common factor. If we factor out <m>3</m>, we have:
          <me>
            -6x+9=3(-2x+3)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We can also consider a common factor of <m>-3</m>, which is often preferred when the leading term has a negative coefficient. When we factor out <m>-3</m>, we have:
          <me>
            -6x+9=-3(2x-3)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Both are factored completely. Using the distributive property, we can verify that both forms are equivalent to the original expression:
          <me>
            3(-2x+3)=3(-2x)+3(3)=-6x+9
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            -3(2x-3)=-3(2x)-3(-3)=-6x+9
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>-6x^2-18x</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <m>6</m> divides both <m>-6</m> and <m>-18</m>, and the highest power of <m>x</m> that divides both <m>x^2</m> and <m>x</m> is <m>x</m>. Because both terms in the original expression are negative, we use <m>-6x</m> as our greatest common factor.
        </p>
        <p>
          When we factor out <m>-6x</m>, both terms inside the parentheses become positive:
          <me>
            -6x^2-18x=-6x(x+3)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We can verify this by distributing <m>-6x</m> back into the binomial:
          <me>
            -6x(x) + (-6x)(3) = -6x^2 - 18x
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>8a^3b-24ab^2+16a^2b^3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are looking for a common factor of <m>8a^3b</m>, <m>-24ab^2</m>, and <m>16a^2b^3</m>.
          First, <m>8</m> divides the coefficients <m>8</m>, <m>-24</m>, and <m>16</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, we consider the variables. The literal part of all three terms contains both <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
          The highest power of <m>a</m> that divides <m>a^3</m>, <m>a</m>, and <m>a^2</m> is <m>a</m>.
          The highest power of <m>b</m> that divides <m>b</m>, <m>b^2</m>, and <m>b^3</m> is <m>b</m>.
          Therefore, our greatest common factor is <m>8ab</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          When we factor out <m>8ab</m>, we obtain:
          <me>
            8a^3b-24ab^2+16a^2b^3=8ab(a^2-3b+2ab^2)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We can use the distributive law to check our solution:
          <me>
            8ab(a^2-3b+2ab^2)=8ab(a^2)+8ab(-3b)+8ab(2ab^2)=8a^3b-24ab^2+16a^2b^3
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          In some cases, it will even be appropriate to use an expression with more than one term as a common factor, much like how <m>ab + ac = a(b+c)</m> uses <m>a</m> as the common factor.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample7">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>3x^2(x-1)+4(x-1)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>(x-1)</m> is a factor of both <m>3x^2(x-1)</m> and <m>4(x-1)</m>. Using the same pattern as above, we can factor this expression into:
          <me>
            (x-1)(3x^2+4)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          In both forms we can see that both the <m>3x^2</m> and <m>4</m> are being distributed to the factor of <m>(x-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Factor by Grouping</title>
    <p>
      If we need to factor an expression with four terms,
      we can look for a common factor that contains more than one term.
      This method is known as factoring by grouping.
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample8">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^3-3x^2+2x-6</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now we are working with an expression with four terms, none of which have a common factor greater than <m>1</m>. We can split our expression into two groups of terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          First, looking at <m>x^3-3x^2</m>, we notice that this expression has a common factor of <m>x^2</m>. When we factor out that term, we are left with <m>x^2(x-3)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, we look at the second group, <m>2x-6</m>. This expression has a common factor of <m>2</m>. When we factor out the <m>2</m>, we are left with <m>2(x-3)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice that we have a common factor now of <m>x-3</m>. We can then factor:
          <me>
            x^3-3x^2+2x-6 = x^2(x-3)+2(x-3) = (x^2+2)(x-3)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We can use the distributive law to check our solution:
          <me>
            (x^2+2)(x-3) = x^2(x)+x^2(-3)+2(x)+2(-3) = x^3-3x^2+2x-6
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="CommonFactorExample9">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the expression <m>ab-6a+2b-12</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Splitting our expression into two groups, let's look at the first group <m>ab-6a</m>. We see that this expression shares a common factor of <m>a</m>, and when we factor that term out, we are left with <m>a(b-6)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second group, <m>2b-12</m>, shares a common factor of <m>2</m>. When we factor that term out, we are left with <m>2(b-6)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both groups share a common factor of <m>b-6</m>; therefore, we can factor:
          <me>
            ab-6a+2b-12 = a(b-6)+2(b-6) = (a+2)(b-6)
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
  </algorithm>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Exercises</title>
    
    <exercises><exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>18x^2-2x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>2x(9x-1)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2t^2+8t</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>9y^3-y^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>y^2(9y-1)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>6ab-4ad+12ac</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>3x^3y^2-18x^2y^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>3x^2y^2(x-6)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>4x^2y^3-18x^5y^2+2xy^4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>5x^2y^3+15x^3y^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>5x^2y^2(y+3x)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>12a^4-21a^3-9a^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>6cd+9df-3cf</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>3(2cd+3df-cf)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>25p^2q-45pq^3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2x^3-10x^2+14x^4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(2x^2)(x-5+7x^2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>-5x^4y+20x^3y^2-20x^2y^3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>18a^2bc+24ab^2c^2-6a^2bc^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>6abc(3a+4bc-ac)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>-p^3-4p^2+p</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>4x^2y^3-18x^5y^2+2xy^4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>2xy^2(2xy-9x^4+y^2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>ax-5x+5a-25</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>3x^3-6x^2+5x-10</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(3x^2+5)(x-2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>10x^2-5x+10x-5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>ac-bc+a^2-ab</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(a-b)(c+a)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>10x^3-2x^2-15x+3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
  </exercises></subsection>
</section>
